WEBVTT

00:00.050 --> 00:00.883
Instructor: In this video,

00:00.883 --> 00:02.820
I'll introduce the git diff command

00:02.820 --> 00:05.730
and we'll also learn how to read,

00:05.730 --> 00:07.950
how to make sense of its output,

00:07.950 --> 00:09.720
which can sometimes be a bit confusing

00:09.720 --> 00:11.370
when you're starting out.

00:11.370 --> 00:16.050
So the git diff command is all about showing changes in git.

00:16.050 --> 00:19.290
So in a given repository, there are many situations

00:19.290 --> 00:21.420
where we might wanna know what changed

00:21.420 --> 00:25.230
between the last commits and our working directory.

00:25.230 --> 00:27.210
What's changed between the staging area

00:27.210 --> 00:28.800
and the working directory?

00:28.800 --> 00:30.660
What's changed between two branches

00:30.660 --> 00:35.310
or two different files over a certain number of commits?

00:35.310 --> 00:37.140
What's changed between what we have locally

00:37.140 --> 00:38.730
versus what's on GitHub?

00:38.730 --> 00:41.850
All these different scenarios where we may want to learn

00:41.850 --> 00:44.250
about changes, about comparisons.

00:44.250 --> 00:46.440
So the git diff command does that.

00:46.440 --> 00:48.300
The git diff command is powerful,

00:48.300 --> 00:50.730
but also sometimes a bit overwhelming

00:50.730 --> 00:52.500
when you're starting out.

00:52.500 --> 00:55.050
It doesn't do anything to the repository,

00:55.050 --> 00:56.460
doesn't impact anything.

00:56.460 --> 00:58.560
Just like git status and git log,

00:58.560 --> 01:00.990
it's purely an informative command.

01:00.990 --> 01:03.840
So it's more about just taking a peek at changes

01:03.840 --> 01:06.480
and trying to understand how things have changed.

01:06.480 --> 01:10.140
Okay, so over here I've got a pretty large git repository.

01:10.140 --> 01:10.980
I'll just show you.

01:10.980 --> 01:12.420
There's a bunch of commits.

01:12.420 --> 01:14.250
There's a decent number of files,

01:14.250 --> 01:17.130
so lots of commits here, lots of files.

01:17.130 --> 01:20.670
It's some HTML, some CSS, some JavaScript.

01:20.670 --> 01:22.530
And currently I haven't changed anything

01:22.530 --> 01:26.220
since the last commit, right on branch master up to date.

01:26.220 --> 01:28.560
Okay, so then let's say I spend

01:28.560 --> 01:30.810
a couple of hours making some changes.

01:30.810 --> 01:34.200
I'm gonna fast forward, but I'll show you an example.

01:34.200 --> 01:35.820
For whatever reason I decide, you know what,

01:35.820 --> 01:38.400
I want this color to be magenta,

01:38.400 --> 01:42.210
and this other color should be, let's go with cyan.

01:42.210 --> 01:45.690
I'm just gonna use labeled named colors

01:45.690 --> 01:47.340
so they're easier to identify.

01:47.340 --> 01:52.110
Instead of, you know, this, let's do coral there.

01:52.110 --> 01:54.420
Anyway, I'll make a bunch of changes.

01:54.420 --> 01:56.820
Maybe I'll also change some HTML.

01:56.820 --> 01:58.650
So in this HTML page,

01:58.650 --> 02:02.250
I'm gonna change this h1 to say, "Hey there."

02:02.250 --> 02:05.070
And then down at the bottom I'll make a paragraph

02:05.070 --> 02:08.570
and it just says, "CHICKENS!!"

02:09.994 --> 02:10.970
All right?

02:10.970 --> 02:13.320
And then I go to this JavaScript file, grid.js,

02:14.400 --> 02:16.170
and somewhere maybe in the middle

02:16.170 --> 02:18.483
I'm going to print out a happy face.

02:20.550 --> 02:23.250
Okay? And then back in a CSS file,

02:23.250 --> 02:28.140
I change some font size from 35 pixels to 65 pixels.

02:28.140 --> 02:30.060
And let's say I do a whole bunch more,

02:30.060 --> 02:33.240
but this is just enough for this demonstration.

02:33.240 --> 02:35.550
And I then realized, you know what?

02:35.550 --> 02:37.680
I should probably make a commit.

02:37.680 --> 02:41.070
I don't remember exactly what I did in each of those files.

02:41.070 --> 02:42.870
So if I just go back and look at this file,

02:42.870 --> 02:45.660
it's not immediately clear what's changed

02:45.660 --> 02:48.240
unless I memorized or I exactly know

02:48.240 --> 02:49.620
what the file looked like.

02:49.620 --> 02:52.560
It's difficult to pinpoint exactly where things changed

02:52.560 --> 02:54.360
across these three files.

02:54.360 --> 02:56.340
So that's where git diff comes in.

02:56.340 --> 02:58.470
The first usage of git diff,

02:58.470 --> 03:02.610
it's just git diff without any additional options.

03:02.610 --> 03:04.410
And this will list all the changes

03:04.410 --> 03:06.540
in our working directory that are not staged

03:06.540 --> 03:08.130
for the next commit.

03:08.130 --> 03:11.850
So it compares the staging area and our working directory.

03:11.850 --> 03:13.320
So I'll show you an example.

03:13.320 --> 03:16.860
Currently I have not staged anything for commits, right?

03:16.860 --> 03:18.183
If I type git status,

03:19.140 --> 03:21.750
we see that there are three modifications,

03:21.750 --> 03:24.180
three different files, none of them staged.

03:24.180 --> 03:26.253
And if I run this git diff command,

03:27.510 --> 03:30.210
we see this kind of crazy looking output.

03:30.210 --> 03:31.800
It's another one of these windows

03:31.800 --> 03:34.110
where I'm not actually in my terminal.

03:34.110 --> 03:35.370
I have to, well, I guess I am,

03:35.370 --> 03:38.820
but I have to escape this view by typing Q,

03:38.820 --> 03:40.860
just like with git log.

03:40.860 --> 03:42.450
So anyway, I see all of this

03:42.450 --> 03:45.570
and it's a little bit crazy looking at first.

03:45.570 --> 03:47.730
And we see some of the stuff that I changed.

03:47.730 --> 03:50.700
We see some red, some green minus and pluses.

03:50.700 --> 03:51.960
But then what is this?

03:51.960 --> 03:56.070
These @ signs, -25,7,

03:56.070 --> 03:58.200
minus, minus, minus, plus, plus, plus.

03:58.200 --> 03:59.460
What's all this stuff go?

03:59.460 --> 04:01.530
I mean, it's just kind of crazy looking

04:01.530 --> 04:05.100
but this is the output of git diff.

04:05.100 --> 04:07.110
So what I'm gonna do in the next video

04:07.110 --> 04:09.090
is walk you through how to read this,

04:09.090 --> 04:11.220
how to make sense of what's going on here,

04:11.220 --> 04:12.810
what's this line doing?

04:12.810 --> 04:14.790
You know, what's the minus and the plus mean

04:14.790 --> 04:16.740
and all that stuff, that's coming up.

04:16.740 --> 04:18.990
But this is just an introduction to the concept.

04:18.990 --> 04:20.280
You make a bunch of changes,

04:20.280 --> 04:22.140
then you want to try and understand more

04:22.140 --> 04:23.700
about what those changes were.

04:23.700 --> 04:26.040
And this is one use case for git diff.

04:26.040 --> 04:28.500
We just don't really know how to make sense of it yet.

04:28.500 --> 04:29.750
So that's the next video.
